# Chapter 8: Evaluation

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:kino, "~> 0.19.0"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1.13"}
])
```

## Overview

The architecture of <a href="06_architecture.livemd">Chapter 6</a> and the rules of Chapters 3 to 5 were each introduced with an argument for why they belong in the prover. This chapter measures what each of them contributes to the number of problems the prover solves, by switching one off at a time against a fixed baseline and running the whole TPTP higher-order corpus at a uniform time budget.

The chapter has three principal results. The contradiction agent $\mathcal{CA}$ accounts for roughly half of all refutations: disabling it costs $364$ of $682$, and nothing else in the matrix is within a factor of four of that. Iterative deepening, demodulation, atom decomposition, finite $o$-quantification and the initial $\gamma$-limit each contribute measurably, and eager definition unfolding improves on the default by $48$ problems. Primitive substitution, the suggestion agent $\mathcal{SA}$, instance-based $\gamma$-expansion, the primitive substitution batch size and the size of the worker pool all lie within the study's noise, which is estimated in <a href="#the-measurement-floor">§ The Measurement Floor</a> at about eight problems on a net figure.

The last of those is the answer to the question <a href="01_introduction.livemd">Chapter 1</a> left open. A pool of one worker per online scheduler performs about three times the inference of a single worker within the same budget and solves two problems fewer. The measurement in <a href="#scaling-with-the-worker-pool">§ Scaling with the Worker Pool</a> locates the reason: the additional inference widens the frontier of open branches without increasing the rate at which global closure is attempted, and global closure is what produces the refutations.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

Every figure in this chapter is scored on refutations. Countermodel answers are reported separately and are not counted as solves, for the reason given in <a href="#scoring">§ Scoring</a>: the prover infers the countermodel verdict from a syntactic test and never checks it, so a `CounterSatisfiable` answer is not attested whether or not it agrees with the TPTP status.

## Method

The corpus is every TH0 and TH1 problem of the TPTP library <a href="#chapter-references">[Sut17]</a>, $5109$ problems in the version used here. Sixteen configurations were run over the full corpus: the baseline, one configuration per component switched off relative to it, and two numeric sweeps at three values each. Each configuration ran once, and each problem received a uniform budget of `timeout` ($2000$ ms). The budget is not a swept dimension, so a difference in solved count between two rows is attributable to the component under ablation rather than to differing time.

The derived tables this chapter reads are in `files/`; the per-problem records they summarise are $81\,744$ rows produced by `ShotTx.Benchmark.TptpRunner` and are held in the implementation repository rather than here.

> [TODO: state the benchmark machine, its core count, and the value `System.schedulers_online()` takes there, since `worker_pool_size` (`:auto`) resolves to it and the whole of the last section is read against that number. Record the sweep dates as well: the baseline ran on 2026-08-15 and the serial configuration on 2026-08-16.]

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

### Scoring

An answer counts as a solve when it agrees with the SZS status <a href="#chapter-references">[Sut08]</a> declared in the problem file. Three further conventions are needed to read the tables.

First, refutations only. `Branch.model_certain?/2` decides the countermodel verdict by a pairwise syntactic test over the literals of a saturated branch, and no countermodel produced in the sweep was ever checked against a model finder: of the $991$ `CounterSatisfiable` answers recorded across the sixteen configurations, none had made a single call to the global closure search. A countermodel answer that agrees with the TPTP status therefore rests on the same unvalidated predicate as one that contradicts it, and neither is evidence about the calculus. Countermodels are reported as a separate column and excluded from the solved counts. Restricting to refutations leaves the ordering of the sixteen configurations unchanged.

Second, agreement with the problem file. Three problems whose header carries no usable status were answered by the prover and are not counted, so the baseline records $743$ solves rather than $746$.

Third, the scoring function itself is narrower than the SZS ontology. `TptpRunner.score/2` scores `Theorem` and `CounterSatisfiable` and records everything else as undecidable, so an answer on an `Unsatisfiable`, `ContradictoryAxioms` or `Satisfiable` problem is neither credited nor counted against the configuration. Re-scoring the sweep under the full table produced no further disagreements, so the counts below are unaffected, but a regression in those classes would not be visible.

```elixir
defmodule Ch8.Data do
  @moduledoc """
  Loader for the derived tables in `files/`. Each file is a comma-separated
  table with a header row and no quoted fields; numeric cells are cast so the
  tables can be sorted and charted without further conversion.
  """

  @dir Path.join(__DIR__, "files")

  def load(name) do
    [header | rows] =
      @dir
      |> Path.join(name)
      |> File.read!()
      |> String.split("\n", trim: true)
      |> Enum.map(&(&1 |> String.trim() |> String.split(",")))

    keys = Enum.map(header, &String.to_atom/1)

    Enum.map(rows, fn cells ->
      keys
      |> Enum.zip(cells)
      |> Map.new(fn {key, value} -> {key, cast(value)} end)
    end)
  end

  defp cast(value) do
    with :error <- parse_integer(value), :error <- parse_float(value) do
      value
    end
  end

  defp parse_integer(value) do
    case Integer.parse(value) do
      {number, ""} -> number
      _other -> :error
    end
  end

  defp parse_float(value) do
    case Float.parse(value) do
      {number, ""} -> number
      _other -> :error
    end
  end
end

summary = Ch8.Data.load("ablation_summary.csv")
Kino.nothing()
```

## Baseline

The baseline is the default `%ShotTx.Data.Parameters{}` of <a href="06_architecture.livemd">Chapter 6</a> with `timeout` set to $2000$ ms: both peer agents present in their default state, every calculus rule enabled, iterative deepening on, and `worker_pool_size` (`:auto`). One departure from the defaults bounds what the chapter can claim: `model_agent_backend` (`:none`) in every one of the sixteen configurations, so $\mathcal{MA}$ ran as an idle process throughout and its contribution is not measured here.

```elixir
Ch8.Data.load("baseline_outcomes.csv")
|> Enum.map(fn row ->
  %{
    "Outcome" => row.outcome,
    "Problems" => row.count,
    "Share (%)" => row.share_pct
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "Baseline outcomes over 5109 problems")
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```text
[%{"Outcome" => "timeout", "Problems" => 3423, "Share (%)" => 67.0}, %{"Outcome" => "thm", "Problems" => 685, "Share (%)" => 13.4}, %{"Outcome" => "parse_timeout", "Problems" => 628, "Share (%)" => 12.3}, %{"Outcome" => "parser_error", "Problems" => 221, "Share (%)" => 4.3}, %{"Outcome" => "no_conjecture", "Problems" => 77, "Share (%)" => 1.5}, %{"Outcome" => "csa", "Problems" => 62, "Share (%)" => 1.2}, %{"Outcome" => "unk", "Problems" => 8, "Share (%)" => 0.2}, %{"Outcome" => "prover_error", "Problems" => 5, "Share (%)" => 0.1}]
```

> `parse_timeout` and `parser_error` are properties of `ShotDs.Tptp` rather than of the prover: the parser never sees `%Parameters{}`, so a problem it cannot read is unreadable under every configuration. `no_conjecture` marks problems that are not attemptable as posed.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

Removing the $628$ parse timeouts, the $221$ parse failures and the $77$ problems without a conjecture leaves $4183$ genuine attempts, of which the baseline solved $743$, or $17.8\,\%$. One problem in six never reached the prover, so the corpus-level rate is bounded by the parser rather than by the calculus, and repairing the parser is the largest single coverage gain available.

```elixir
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

cumulative =
  "baseline_solved.csv"
  |> Ch8.Data.load()
  |> Enum.sort_by(& &1.time_ms)
  |> Enum.with_index(1)
  |> Enum.map(fn {row, rank} -> %{"time_ms" => max(row.time_ms, 1), "solved" => rank} end)

# Vega-Lite names this curve "step-after". The atom :step_after serialises with
# the underscore intact, which is not a curve name, and the line layer is then
# dropped without an error.
time_scale = [type: :log, domain: [1, 3200], nice: false]

Vl.new(
  width: 560,
  height: 270,
  title: [
    text: "Baseline: solves accumulated against wall clock",
    subtitle: "743 answers agreeing with TPTP, cumulative",
    anchor: :start,
    font_size: 13,
    subtitle_font_size: 11,
    subtitle_color: "#6b7280"
  ]
)
|> Vl.layers([
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.data_from_values(cumulative)
  |> Vl.mark(:line, interpolate: "step-after", color: "#2c5fa8", stroke_width: 2)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "time_ms",
    type: :quantitative,
    scale: time_scale,
    axis: [grid: false, title: "wall clock in milliseconds, logarithmic"]
  )
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "solved",
    type: :quantitative,
    scale: [domain: [0, 780], nice: false],
    axis: [grid: true, grid_color: "#e5e7eb", title: "problems solved"]
  ),
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.data_from_values([%{"time_ms" => 2000}])
  |> Vl.mark(:rule, color: "#8a939c", stroke_dash: [4, 3])
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "time_ms", type: :quantitative, scale: time_scale),
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.data_from_values([%{"time_ms" => 2000, "solved" => 700, "label" => "budget"}])
  |> Vl.mark(:text, align: :right, dx: -5, color: "#6b7280", font_size: 10)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "time_ms", type: :quantitative, scale: time_scale)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "solved", type: :quantitative)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:text, "label", type: :nominal)
])
|> Vl.config(view: [stroke: nil])
```

